   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law, in relation to personal phone calls for\ninmates in certain circumstances",
    "basePrintNo": "S1850",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S1850-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 25,
       "fullName": "Velmanette Montgomery",
       "imgName": "381_velmanette_montgomery.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 381,
       "sessionMemberId": 1195,
       "sessionYear": 2019,
       "shortName": "MONTGOMERY"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §137, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S1850\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to personal phone calls\nfor inmates in certain circumstances\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo allow state prison inmates entering solitary confinement in special\nhousing units (SHU) and in residential mental health treatment units to\nmake a telephone call upon admission into SHU and at least once a week\nthereafter.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection I of the bill amends subdivision six of section 137 of the\ncorrection law to allow an inmate to make a telephone call upon his or\nher admission into solitary confinement, keeplock, a residential mental\nhealth treatment program and administrative segregation and at least\nonce per week thereafter.\n\nSection II sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThere are approximately 4,000 inmates confined to special housing units\n(SHU) within the state prison system as a result of disciplinary sanc-\ntions. Currently, inmates being held in SHU on such sanctions are\nconfined to a cell for 23 hours per day, with an hour of outdoor recre-\nation in a fenced-in pen or cage, and lose other privileges including\naccess to telephone calls, personal property, packages and commissary.\n\nSHU inmates are limited to one visit per week or may be prohibited from\nreceiving any visits at all. For a sanction which exceeds six months,\ninmates are often transferred to a dedicated SHU facility in either\nChemung or Franklin counties. Since these facilities are far from the\nurban and suburban communities where most inmates' families reside,\ntransfers often result in few, or no, visits during an inmate's SHU\nconfinement. Visits have become even less frequent since the elimination\nof the free bus program from the state budget.\n\nThe impact of solitary confinement on the family of an inmate is a\nsignificant burden in its own right. A parent, spouse or child of the\ninmate often has no idea of what has happened to his or her loved one\nwhen telephone calls suddenly cease.\n\nWhile an inmate has a constitutional right to correspondence, there are\ntimes when postage, paper and writing implements are not present, and\ncorrespondence to and from the correctional facility may be delayed or\nunreliable. Permitting inmates to make telephone calls,upop admission\ninto SHU and at weekly intervals ensures that family members'are not\nleft wondering what has happened to their loved ones.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2016: S985 Montgomery/A4620 Rozic. Passed Assembly.\n\n2018: S8646 Sepulveda/A1208 Rozic. Referred to Crime Victims, Crime and\nCorrection.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPACT ON THE STATE:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
